    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change mirrors the existing sibling feature (`CheckOwnerCityCapacity` / `CityOwnerLimitExceededException` / `CityOwnerLimitExceededExceptionHandler`) almost exactly, and that's the right call here:\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** `CheckOwnerDailyLimit` is its own guard step, consistent with the one-check-per-class pattern already used for telephone/household/city gates. It declares only what it needs (`OwnerRepository`), correctly omitting `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` since the daily count doesn't depend on the request body \u2014 matching AGENTS.md's \"declare only what the step needs.\"\n- **Naming and conventions match.** `service` method name, `static final DAILY_LIMIT` constant with `{@value}` javadoc, the checked exception extending `Exception`, and the handler using the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper all follow the established idiom rather than the generic AGENTS.md worked example.\n- **No inappropriate duplication.** The `findAll().stream().filter().count()` shape recurs from `CheckOwnerCityCapacity`, but the two filter on different criteria and, unlike the city count (which was extracted to `countInCity` only because `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` shares it), this count has no second consumer \u2014 so inlining is the right choice, not a missed extraction.\n- **Wiring is coherent.** The step is inserted among the other capacity/uniqueness gates before `build`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors, and the escalation YAML follows the standard fully-qualified-name convention. No class or method is turning into a catch-all.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this natural.",
